ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [wazero's fixed-seed-42 + frozen-clock defaults are documented design choices for deterministic replay/testing, not a hidden bug — the observed 30/30 key collision is the *expected* output of a seeded PRNG, not an emergent ABI failure; the ~31x clock-decoupling factor is just (real syscall latency)/(fake +1ms increment), an arithmetic identity not a measured phenomenon]
CONFOUNDS: [RE-1's "spec violation" rests on interpreting "high-quality" + the blocking clause as synonymous with CSPRNG — but preview1 prose never names CSPRNG; the successor spec's explicit "cryptographically secure" + the "deterministic environments MUST OMIT" clause is retrospectively projected onto preview1, conflating two spec generations; Rust getrandom's doc string is a *consumer assumption*, not a preview1 normative requirement]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [that "unable to immediately provide sufficient high-quality random data" entails CSPRNG semantics rather than merely "non-degenerate entropy source"; that wasmtime/wasmer's CSPRNG defaults reflect spec-compliance rather than independent product choices; that the curated 7/10 sample generalizes to the population of WASI hosts; that a guest CANNOT defend (no L1 test of explicit guest-side entropy mixing or rejection-of-deterministic-host)]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [n=30 cross-process + n=10 cross-language are sufficient for the determinism claim (p≈0 under any non-degenerate null) but RE-2 prevalence is explicitly signal-not-measurement — no CI, no denominator, no blast-radius quantification; the "31x" clock-decoupling has no variance reported across runs/platforms; no test of whether wazero's behavior varies by version (only v1.12.0 sampled), so "wazero is THE anomaly" is a point-estimate not a trajectory]
RATIONALE: The CSPRNG-contract reading (RE-1) is defensible but not airtight on preview1's literal prose — the decisive "cryptographically secure" + "MUST OMIT" language is successor-spec, and the orchestrator's own residual #1 concedes this. The cross-runtime anomaly (RE-3) is genuine and well-evidenced (verbatim source from all three runtimes). The synthesis is real but the SWC-120/CrowdSec/wetware practitioner-finds materially deflate the novelty claim from "discovered hazard" to "documented synthesis of known hazard" — a contribution, but YELLOW-altitude not GREEN. Combined with RE-2 being signal-not-prevalence (auth-gated, curated n=7), the theory case is sound but the empirical altitude is one L-stage short. An L2 with (a) authenticated prevalence + denominator, (b) explicit preview1-vs-successor spec-violation framing, and (c) guest-side defensibility test would convert this to GREEN. Honest yellow, not red — the mechanism is real, the residuals are altitude not validity.
